Any import starting with com.fasterxml.jackson is wrong. Replace with tools.jackson.

EXCEPTION CLASSES — Spring Boot 4 ships NO application-level exceptions:
  ResourceNotFoundException, PaymentGatewayException, etc. must be separate .java files
  in the .exception package. Never declare them inline. If you reference one that is not
  in the dependency files, add the correct import (com.{pkg}.exception.ClassName) — the
  standalone file will be generated; do NOT inline it as a nested class.

JPA BIDIRECTIONAL SAVE — set the child's owning-side reference BEFORE saving the parent.
  When a parent owns a @OneToMany(cascade=…) collection whose child holds the @ManyToOne +
  @JoinColumn(nullable=false), populating parent.setChildren(list) and then repo.save(parent)
  inserts every child with a NULL foreign key and the request 500s at runtime. Always set the
  back-reference first, e.g.:
      order.setItems(orderItems);
      orderItems.forEach(i -> i.setOrder(order));   // REQUIRED before the save
      orderRepository.save(order);                  // cascade now writes a valid order_id

JPA SEEDING — never assign an @Id that is @GeneratedValue. In a CommandLineRunner/seeder,
  construct each entity with the no-arg constructor + setters (or a builder) and leave the id
  null so Hibernate generates it. Passing an explicit id (e.g. new Trainer(UUID.randomUUID(), …))
  makes Spring Data see a non-null id, treat the row as detached, and issue an UPDATE (merge) that
  matches zero rows — the seeder crashes at boot with ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException and
  the app restart-loops. Seed every entity the way its siblings do: no id argument.

PRINCIPAL IS NOT AN ID — @AuthenticationPrincipal UserDetails.getUsername() is the login subject
  (the email/username), NOT a UUID or numeric id. Never UUID.fromString(userDetails.getUsername())
  or otherwise parse it as an entity id. Resolve the user through the repository
  (userRepository.findByEmail(userDetails.getUsername())) and use that entity's id.

8. UI COMPONENTS — shadcn-first. Follow this priority order for frontend files:
   (a) PREFER shadcn: for any element shadcn provides — Button, Input, Textarea, Label, Select,
       Checkbox, RadioGroup, Switch, Form, Dialog, AlertDialog, DropdownMenu, Popover, Tooltip,
       Sheet, Table, Card, Badge, Separator, Tabs, Accordion, Avatar, Skeleton, Calendar, and
       Toast (via sonner) — import from @/components/ui/<name>, ALWAYS lowercase
       (@/components/ui/select, NEVER @/components/ui/Select). These files are pre-installed; the
       UI INVENTORY section lists each one's exact exports — use those names verbatim.
   (b) Radix ONLY as fallback: import from @radix-ui/react-* directly ONLY when a primitive is
       absent from the UI INVENTORY. Never reach for raw radix for anything shadcn already wraps.
   (c) Layout and static elements: use plain HTML tags with Tailwind CSS classes.
   (d) Toast: import { toast } from 'sonner' — never react-toastify or a use-toast hook.
   NEVER import from @/lib/utils in app files. Use clsx for className merging.

# TYPESCRIPT / REACT 19 CONSTRAINTS (TS/TSX files only — every rule fixes a recurring build breaker)
1. TANSTACK QUERY v5 API ONLY (v4 idioms do not compile):
   - useQuery({ queryKey: ['trainers'], queryFn: fetchTrainers })  — OBJECT form; positional
     useQuery(['trainers'], fetchTrainers) is a compile error in v5.
   - Mutations expose isPending — mutation.isLoading DOES NOT EXIST on useMutation results.
   - useMutation({ mutationFn: createTrainer, onSuccess: ... }) — object form here too.
2. ZOD NUMERIC FORM FIELDS: HTML inputs yield strings — use z.coerce.number() (NOT z.number())
   for any numeric field bound to an <input>, or the zodResolver output type will not match
   the DTO interface and the resolver assignment fails to compile.
3. TYPE RE-EXPORTS: isolatedModules is on — re-exporting a type requires
   export type { Foo } from './bar'  (a plain export { Foo } fails to compile).
4. BOOLEAN JSX ATTRIBUTES: allowFullScreen, disabled, checked take booleans —
   allowFullScreen={true} or bare allowFullScreen, never allowFullScreen="true".
5. API TYPES AND SERVICES ARE PRE-GENERATED GROUND TRUTH: frontend/src/types/<domain>.ts
   and frontend/src/services/<domain>Service.ts are DERIVED from the backend contract and
   already exist. Their real content appears in your dependency context: import the
   exact exported interfaces and service functions shown there. ADMIN API functions live
   in the same domain service as the public ones (admin-prefixed on name collisions) —
   there is no adminXService module; the API SDK section of the contract card is the
   complete function list. NEVER call axios/apiClient directly from pages, hooks, or
   components — always call the generated service functions. Frontend-only types that
   have no backend counterpart (cart state, form view-models) go in
   frontend/src/types/local/<name>.ts; non-API service modules (localStorage persistence)
   go in frontend/src/services/local/<name>.ts.

   THE DERIVED FILES WIN OVER THE INSTRUCTION — for NAMES and for LOCATIONS alike. The
   instruction was written before these files were derived, so wherever it contradicts a
   types/services file in your dependency context, the DERIVED FILE is correct and the
   instruction is stale.
   (a) FIELD AND TYPE NAMES: if the instruction says a card shows `durationMonths` but the
       type declares `durationInMonths`, write durationInMonths; if it names a field the type
       does not declare (e.g. features), that field DOES NOT EXIST — omit it, or render from
       fields that do exist.
   (b) WHICH MODULE EXPORTS A FUNCTION: the instruction may say "bookingService exports
       getAllFitnessClasses" while the SDK card shows classService exporting it. The CARD is
       correct. Import each symbol from the module that actually exports it — splitting one
       instructed import across several modules when that is where the functions live. If a
       named module does not export what the instruction claims, find the module that does;
       NEVER hand-write an apiClient/axios call to work around the missing export, and never
       invent an endpoint path from a DTO name.
